<article class="env-definition" id="05E6">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05E6">Definition <span data-tag="05E6">15.90.1</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. Let $M$ be an $R$-module. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
Let $I \subset R$ be an ideal. We say $M$ is an <em>$I$-power torsion module</em> if for every $m \in M$ there exists an $n > 0$ such that $I^ n m = 0$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Let $f \in R$. We say $M$ is <em>an $f$-power torsion module</em> if for each $m \in M$, there exists an $n > 0$ such that $f^ n m = 0$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>